Let d ≥ 2 be an integer and let E = {±e_1, …, ±e_d} denote the set of unit vectors of Z^d. A cookie environment is a family ω = (ω(z, ·, ·))_{z ∈ Z^d}, where for each site z ∈ Z^d and each i ∈ N, ω(z, ·, i) is a probability vector on E (the "i-th cookie" at site z). Such an environment is sampled according to a probability measure P satisfying:

(IID) the family (ω(z, ·, ·))_{z ∈ Z^d} is i.i.d. under P;

(UEL) (uniform ellipticity) there exists κ > 0 such that, for all z ∈ Z^d, e ∈ E, and i ∈ N, P-a.s. ω(z, e, i) ≥ κ;

(BD) (bounded number of non-placebo cookies) there exists a deterministic M ∈ N such that, for all z ∈ Z^d, P-a.s. ω(z, ·, i) equals the uniform distribution on E for every i > M.

Given a realization of ω, the excited random walk (ERW) starting at 0 is the process (X_n)_{n ≥ 0} on Z^d with X_0 = 0 such that, conditionally on the past, if the walker is at z ∈ Z^d and this is its i-th visit to z, it jumps to z + e with probability ω(z, e, i). Let P_0 denote the resulting annealed law of (X_n) starting from 0.

For a nonzero direction ℓ ∈ R^d \ {0}, define the event
A_ℓ := { lim_{n → ∞} X_n · ℓ = ∞ }.

Question: Under the assumptions (IID), (UEL), and (BD), does the zero-one law
P_0[A_ℓ] ∈ {0, 1}
hold for every ℓ ∈ R^d \ {0}? More generally, identify conditions on the cookie environment that imply this dichotomy.

